{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"## Fix pushed: Gerrit 3252 (commit 215c1ba546e)\n\n**Reproduced first (2026-11-02, sandbox).** In a fresh repo with `origin` and `gerrit`, `cp -R` of a linked worktree followed by `git -C <copy> remote remove <r>` for each remote left `git remote -v` in the main checkout empty: `git -C <copy> rev-parse --git-common-dir` resolved to the main repo's `.git`. Running the repository `Makefile`'s `make worktree` there printed git's raw \"'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\". This confirms the mechanism recorded in the 2026-10-27 comment.\n\n**Change.**\n- `Makefile` `worktree` recipe: `git remote get-url origin` runs before `git fetch`. If `origin` is missing, the recipe prints an `ERROR` naming it, the `git remote add origin https://github.com/navapbc/rebar.git` command, and the runbook pointer, then exits 1 without fetching. If `gerrit` is missing, it prints a non-fatal `WARNING` that points to the runbook.\n- `docs/local-dev-env.md`: new section \"Restoring the origin and gerrit remotes\" with the `origin` URL and both documented `gerrit` forms (HTTPS and SSH), restore commands, and the copied-worktree cause.\n- `tests/unit/test_makefile_worktree.py`: 5 new tests (missing origin stops before fetch with no raw git text; restore command and runbook printed; missing gerrit warns and still succeeds; no warning when both are present; runbook records the URLs).\n\n**Evidence.** The 4 behavior tests were RED against the old recipe (the missing-origin test failed on git's raw message), and all 13 in the file passed after the change. A real-repo sandbox run showed the named ERROR with no remotes, and a warning followed by a normal fetch with only `origin`. `make lint` exit 0, `make typecheck` clean (753 files), and 750 doc/Makefile-related unit tests passed. `make verify`: the default tier passed (20426 passed, 27 skipped). The integration tier had 1 failure, `test_s3_doctor.py::test_git_remote_s3_api_surface_contract` (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'git_remote_s3'`), the known host failure tracked as `dominating-effete-bat`. So the `make verify` AC is not ticked yet.\n\nThe general copied-worktree hazard is filed as `asthmatic-grave-caudata` (discovered_from this bug)."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793685873191808001,"uuid":"13c494e3-4603-40f8-aaa0-9a22d8e22bce"}